From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mason Morris <masonmorris8@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 22:30:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 11/11] Serialize the context lifecycle and keep the render
 thread alive

The embedder's lifecycle calls are async, and a device follower moves
contexts from its own thread, so suspend, resume, close and setSinkId
race. Each now decides under the backend manager lock, and the context
tracks whether its stream is paused:

- close shakes hands with any live stream, including one whose Startup
  is still pending, and sends Close past a pending suspend's staging,
  which used to hold it forever
- only the latest suspend pauses the stream, so one that a resume or a
  close overtook leaves it alone
- resume runs after a suspend the render thread has not answered yet
- a second suspend keeps the messages the first one staged, and resume
  replays them before anything sent meanwhile
- setSinkId replays cached messages through the sender it holds, which
  deadlocked; keeps the graph on the previous device, or on none, when
  the new stream fails; and gives up after 2 s on a stream that runs no
  callback, leaving the context as it was

The render capacity worker also exits when its stop sender is dropped,
and the Chromium HRTF convolver zeroes its DC and Nyquist imaginary
parts, which a non-finite sample made the inverse FFT reject with a
panic on the render thread.
